Validation must be real when possible, server side, staged, and evidence-driven:
A. Ensure nonempty syntactically valid HTTPS URL. Reject unrecognized protocols, credentials in URL, localhost, private/link-local IPs, IP literals where unsafe, DNS rebinding, unsafe redirects, oversized responses, non-HTML/JSON/YAML as appropriate, and unsafe content. Fetch with timeout, size/redirect limits; revalidate every redirect and resolved destination. Avoid SSRF. Never have browser fetch arbitrary docs directly.
B. Check HTTP status and content. Parse OpenAPI when present; for human-readable docs analyze presence of substantive API reference content (endpoint methods/paths, authentication, request/response examples, structured schema or reference links). A website mentioning “API” is not automatically valid.
C. Ask the AI documentation classifier to provide a structured evidence result with valid, provider, baseUrlCandidates, documentedEndpoints, authEvidence, paginationEvidence, sourceLinks, warnings, and reasons; accept only references actually grounded in fetched data. Validation is not simply regex on the hostname.
D. Show green Valid API documentation: Calendly API v2 when evidence is sufficient, with extracted endpoint count/actual details and citations. When source is incomplete, say Partially validated — missing required evidence and block until user supplements docs or explicitly chooses a sandbox-only fixture route.
E. Example negative test: https://google.com should show “This appears to be a general website, not an API reference. Please provide developer/API documentation containing endpoints, authentication and response formats.” and Proceed stays disabled. Also test malformed URL, 404, timeout, empty content, suspicious redirect.
