HUMAN-IN-THE-LOOP CONTROLS / CHECKPOINTING
At minimum the run pauses for mandatory human review:
- Approval #1 — API capabilities & field assumptions, including unavailable usage fields.
- Approval #2 — Auth method, scopes and permissions before generating auth code.
- Approval #3 — Generated code/security findings before marking package complete.
An approval card includes actual evidence, requested action, affected artifacts, risk level, Approve, Request Changes (with feedback), Reject, and link to traces. Approve advances; Request Changes re-runs only affected stages; Reject marks blocked; no automatic approval. Changes must be saved to the run/audit trail.

Persistent run controls:
- Pause: do not begin new stages. Safely finish or checkpoint the current atomic subtask; show “Pausing after current checkpoint” if an in-flight provider call cannot be interrupted atomically. Never falsely claim mid-token resumption.
- Resume: restart from the most recent durable checkpoint (per stage/subtask/file). Do not regenerate prior successful stages or lose already saved files, approvals, selected model or progress, even after browser refresh. For a partially completed model call that can't resume midstream, rerun that single unfinished subtask, not the entire workflow. Make it obvious what will resume next.
- Cancel: confirm permanent termination of the run; abort pending tasks, disable resume, retain audit traces and already created draft artifacts as versioned records. Distinguish Cancel Run from Delete Integration.
- Retry failed step: retry only the failing stage after actionable remediation; preserve previous checkpoints and trace history.
Define an explicit server-side state machine: draft → validating → awaiting_model → queued → running → awaiting_approval → paused → running → generated → validated → ready_for_review; other terminal states failed, cancelled, and blocked. Enforce allowable transitions and idempotent commands. Save checkpoints after successful endpoints, mappings, auth plan, each generated file, and reviews. Use operation IDs/version tokens to avoid double-generation on repeated clicks. Run state should survive route changes and refresh; if platform persistence cannot support refresh, surface this limitation honestly.
